#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM5020", unit: "U4")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U4.1 Actions",[Verify that $S_n$ acts on $\{1,dots,n\}$ by evaluation.],[Check identity and composition.],[The operation in $S_n$ is function composition.],[The identity permutation fixes every element. Also $(sigma tau) dot i=sigma(tau(i))=sigma dot(tau dot i)$ by the definition of composition. These are exactly the action axioms.],[Explicitly check both axioms.]),
 p(2,"E","U4.1 Stabilizers",[Find the stabilizer of one in the natural $S_4$ action and its order.],[It permutes the other three letters freely.],[It is isomorphic to $S_3$.],[The stabilizer contains exactly the permutations fixing one. Restriction to $\{2,3,4\}$ identifies it with $S_3$, so it has six elements. Orbit–stabilizer agrees: $24/6=4$.],[Give the subgroup description as well as its size.]),
 p(3,"E","U4.2 Conjugacy",[Compute $(123)(12)(123)^(-1)$.],[Conjugation relabels a cycle.],[Send one to two and two to three.],[The result is $(23)$, by the formula $tau(a b)tau^(-1)=(tau(a)tau(b))$.],[Identify the conjugating permutation and its two relevant images.]),
 p(4,"E","U4.2 Simplicity",[Which nontrivial finite cyclic groups are simple?],[Every subgroup of an abelian group is normal.],[A cyclic group of composite order has a proper nontrivial subgroup.],[Exactly the cyclic groups of prime order. Prime order permits no proper nontrivial subgroup by Lagrange. For composite order $n=a b$ with $a,b>1$, the subgroup generated by $g^a$ has order $b$, hence is proper, nontrivial, and normal.],[Address both prime and composite orders.]),
 p(5,"C","U4.1 Kernel of an action",[Show the kernel of the homomorphism associated with an action is the intersection of all stabilizers.],[Both descriptions say an element fixes every point.],[Write the two membership conditions explicitly.],[An element $g$ is in the homomorphism kernel exactly when its associated permutation is the identity, meaning $g dot x=x$ for every $x$. This is equivalent to membership in every $G_x$, so the kernel equals $inter_(x in X)G_x$. It is normal because it is a kernel.],[Explain the equivalence point by point.]),
 p(6,"C","U4.1 Conjugate stabilizers",[Prove $G_(h dot x)=h G_x h^(-1)$.],[Apply $h^(-1)$ to the equation defining a stabilizer.],[The condition is equivalent to $h^(-1)g h$ fixing $x$.],[$g dot(h dot x)=h dot x$ holds exactly when $(h^(-1)g h)dot x=x$. Thus $g$ stabilizes $h dot x$ exactly when $h^(-1)g h in G_x$, which is equivalent to $g in h G_x h^(-1)$.],[Prove equality through an if-and-only-if membership calculation.]),
 p(7,"C","U4.2 Class equation",[Use conjugacy classes to show the center of $S_3$ is trivial.],[List its cycle types.],[The class sizes are one, three, and two.],[The identity is a singleton class. The three transpositions form one class and the two three-cycles another, by cycle-type conjugacy. Only elements in singleton conjugacy classes are central, so $Z(S_3)=\{e\}$.],[Explain why a singleton conjugacy class characterizes a central element.]),
 p(8,"C","U4.2 Prime-power groups",[Prove a group of order $p^2$ is abelian.],[The center has order divisible by $p$.],[If its order were $p$, the quotient by the center would be cyclic.],[The class equation gives a nontrivial center, whose order is $p$ or $p^2$ by Lagrange. In the latter case the group is abelian. In the former, $G slash Z(G)$ has prime order and is cyclic; #link(label("MM5020-U3.09"))[U3.09] then makes $G$ abelian, forcing the center to be all of $G$ and contradicting its supposed order $p$.],[Treat both possible center sizes and justify the cyclic-quotient step.]),
 p(9,"C","U4.1 Orbit counting",[Count two-colorings of four labeled square vertices up to rotation, with reflections excluded.],[Use the cyclic rotation group of order four.],[The four rotations fix $16,2,4,2$ colorings respectively.],[The identity fixes all $2^4=16$. A quarter or three-quarter rotation forces all vertices to share one color, giving two each. A half-turn permits independent colors on two opposite pairs, giving four. The orbit count is $(16+2+4+2)/4=6$.],[Use precisely the rotation group requested and explain each fixed-point count.]),
 p(10,"A","U4.2 Simple prime-power groups",[Classify finite simple groups whose order is a power of a prime.],[Their center is a nontrivial normal subgroup.],[Simplicity forces the center to be the whole group.],[For order $p^n$ with $n>=1$, the center is nontrivial by the class equation. It is normal, so simplicity forces $Z(G)=G$. Thus $G$ is abelian. A nontrivial finite abelian simple group is cyclic of prime order, so here the only possibility is $C_p$. Conversely $C_p$ is simple.],[Prove the converse and exclude all higher prime powers.]),
 p(11,"A","U4.1 Coset actions",[Let $H$ have finite index $n$ in $G$. Show there is a normal subgroup $N subset.eq H$ with $[G:N]<=n!$.],[Let $G$ act on its left cosets of $H$.],[Use the kernel of the resulting map to $S_n$.],[Left multiplication defines an action on the $n$ cosets. Let $N$ be its kernel; it is normal. An element of $N$ fixes the coset $H$, so it belongs to $H$. The first isomorphism theorem identifies $G slash N$ with the image subgroup of $S_n$, so its order is at most $n!$. This also proves the quotient is finite even if $G$ is infinite.],[Do not assume $H$ itself is normal or $G$ finite.]),
 p(12,"A","U4.2 Faithful actions of simple groups",[Prove every nontrivial action of a simple group is faithful. Deduce that $A_5$ cannot act nontrivially on a set of four elements.],[The kernel of an action is normal.],[A faithful action on four points would embed $A_5$ into $S_4$.],[The action kernel is either trivial or the whole simple group. A nontrivial action rules out the latter, so it is faithful. Since $A_5$ is simple of order sixty, a nontrivial four-point action would give an injection into $S_4$, which has only twenty-four elements, impossible.],[Here “nontrivial action” means at least one group element moves at least one point.]),
)
